### What is the Fibonacci number problem?

The Fibonacci sequence starts with 0 and 1, and each subsequent number is the sum of the two preceding numbers. The problem asks you to compute the nth Fibonacci number efficiently.

This is the classic introduction to dynamic programming. The naive recursive solution is correct but exponentially slow, and fixing it demonstrates the core DP principle: solve each subproblem once.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class FibIntro {

    // Naive: exponential
    public static long fibNaive(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        return fibNaive(n - 1) + fibNaive(n - 2);
    }

    // Memoized: linear time, linear space
    public static long fibMemo(int n, long[] cache) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (cache[n] != -1) return cache[n];
        return cache[n] = fibMemo(n - 1, cache) + fibMemo(n - 2, cache);
    }

    // Rolling: linear time, constant space
    public static long fibRolling(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        long prev2 = 0, prev1 = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            long cur = prev1 + prev2;
            prev2 = prev1;
            prev1 = cur;
        }
        return prev1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long[] cache = new long[51];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(cache, -1);
        System.out.println("Naive  fib(10) = " + fibNaive(10));
        System.out.println("Memo   fib(50) = " + fibMemo(50, cache));
        System.out.println("Rolling fib(50) = " + fibRolling(50));
    }
}
```

```text
Naive  fib(10) = 55
Memo   fib(50) = 12586269025
Rolling fib(50) = 12586269025
```

### Why is naive recursion exponentially slow?

The naive recursion creates a binary call tree. Each call makes two further calls, and many subproblems are computed multiple times. Computing fib(5) calls fib(3) twice and fib(2) three times. The total work is approximately O(2^n), growing by the golden ratio (~1.618) at each level.

Computing fib(40) takes over a billion calls, and fib(50) takes minutes on modern hardware. This exponential growth makes the naive version impractical for any input beyond about 40.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class FibCallCount {
    private static long calls = 0;

    public static long fib(int n) {
        calls++;
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        calls = 0;
        fib(30);
        System.out.println("fib(30) calls: " + calls);

        calls = 0;
        fib(40);
        System.out.println("fib(40) calls: " + calls);
    }
}
```

```text
fib(30) calls: 2692537
fib(40) calls: 331160281
```

### How does memoization fix the problem?

Memoization stores each computed value the first time it is calculated and returns the cached result on subsequent calls. The check-and-cache pattern ensures each unique subproblem is solved exactly once, collapsing the exponential call tree to a linear chain.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FibMemoMap {

    private static final Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public static long fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);
        long result = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
        memo.put(n, result);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("fib(50) = " + fib(50));
        System.out.println("Cache size: " + memo.size());
    }
}
```

```text
fib(50) = 12586269025
Cache size: 49
```

### How does the bottom-up rolling approach work?

The bottom-up approach starts from the base cases and iterates forward. Since each value depends only on the previous two, we keep just two variables and slide the window.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class FibRolling {

    public static long fib(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return 0;
        if (n == 1) return 1;
        long twoBack = 0, oneBack = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            long current = oneBack + twoBack;
            twoBack = oneBack;
            oneBack = current;
        }
        return oneBack;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("fib(10) = " + fib(10));
        System.out.println("fib(92) = " + fib(92));
    }
}
```

```text
fib(10) = 55
fib(92) = 7540113804746346429
```

### How is memoization different from tabulation?

| Aspect | Memoization | Tabulation | Rolling |
|---|---|---|---|
| Direction | Top-down | Bottom-up | Bottom-up |
| Space | O(n) cache + stack | O(n) array | O(1) two variables |
| Stack risk | StackOverflow for large n | None | None |
| Subproblems | Only reachable ones | All from 0 to n | All from 0 to n |
| Best for | Sparse state spaces | Dense, sequential | When only final value needed |

### What about integer overflow?

Fibonacci numbers grow exponentially. The 92nd Fibonacci number is 7,540,113,804,746,346,429, which fits in a long. The 93rd is 12,200,160,415,121,876,738, which exceeds Long.MAX_VALUE and overflows silently.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigInteger;

public class FibBigInt {

    public static BigInteger fib(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return BigInteger.ZERO;
        if (n == 1) return BigInteger.ONE;
        BigInteger a = BigInteger.ZERO, b = BigInteger.ONE;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            BigInteger c = a.add(b);
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
        return b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("fib(93) = " + fib(93));
    }
}
```

```text
fib(93) = 12200160415121876738
```

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting both base cases causes infinite recursion or wrong results.

```java
// WRONG: only one base case
public static long fib(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);  // fib(1) calls fib(0) + fib(-1) endlessly
}

// CORRECT: both base cases
public static long fib(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    if (n == 1) return 1;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}
```

Using `int` instead of `long` causes overflow at fib(47). Always use `long` at minimum.

### When should you use this in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the analytics team projects a loyalty reward balance where each month's points equal the sum of the previous two months. Customers query their projected balance at any future month.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigInteger;

public class RewardProjector {

    public static BigInteger project(int month) {
        if (month < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Month must be non-negative");
        if (month == 0) return BigInteger.ZERO;
        if (month == 1) return BigInteger.ONE;
        BigInteger a = BigInteger.ZERO, b = BigInteger.ONE;
        for (int i = 2; i <= month; i++) {
            BigInteger c = a.add(b);
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
        return b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Month 12: " + project(12) + " points");
        System.out.println("Month 100: " + project(100) + " points");
    }
}
```

```text
Month 12: 144 points
Month 100: 354224848179261915075 points
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that the naive Fibonacci is the textbook example of overlapping subproblems. The call tree has O(2^n) nodes but only O(n) distinct states, which is exactly the condition that makes DP profitable.

Matrix exponentiation can compute fib(n) in O(log n) time, and Binet's formula gives a closed-form using the golden ratio, though it suffers from floating-point precision loss for large n.

The pattern generalises to any linear recurrence with constant coefficients, and the window size determines how many rolling variables are needed.
